# Photosensitizing metal-organic layers for photocatalysis, artificial photosynthesis and fluorescence imaging

An Li†, Yufeng Zhang†, Zichen Sun, Zhiyang Niu & Guangxu Lan
Beijing National Laboratory for Molecular Science, State Key Laboratory of Rare Earth Materials Chemistry and Applications, College of Chemistry and Molecular Engineering, Peking University, Beijing 100084, China
In recent years, metal-organic layers (MOLs) with high-density and accessible open sites have emerged as a two-dimensional version of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with various potential applications. Particularly, MOLs represent a promising platform for photocatalysis, artificial photosynthesis, and fluorescence imaging through the hierarchical assembly of photosensitizers and catalysts or other functional groups into MOLs. This review provides an overview of the structural design and synthesis strategies of MOLs with a particular emphasis on the applications of photosensitizing MOLs, illustrating the advantages of the MOLs-based material. The final part discusses perspectives on the challenges encountered in this field and the emerging developments that can be expected.
## metal-organic layers, metal-organic frameworks, photocatalysis, artificial photosynthesis
Citation: Li A, Zhang Y, Sun Z, Niu Z, Lan G. Photosensitizing metal-organic layers for photocatalysis, artificial photosynthesis and fluorescence imaging. Sci China Chem, 2023, 66: 3372–3382, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-023-1784-8
## 1 Introduction
Two-dimensional (2D) materials have attracted considerable research interest since the development of graphene [1]. For a long time, the effectiveness of conventional two-dimensional materials in photocatalysis has been relatively limited. One aspect of this limitation is due to the relatively fixed structure of two-dimensional materials. Additionally, during the synthesis process, it is challenging to incorporate photosensitizers or other active groups, leading to low light absorption capabilities. Furthermore, conventional two-dimensional materials require functionalization to meet the evolving demands for material properties. Consequently, the development of molecular two-dimensional materials with synthetic flexibility and tunability is important.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are an emerging class of materials for photocatalysis [2–4]. As a 2D monolayered version of MOFs [5], metal-organic layers (MOLs) inherit characteristics of both MOFs and 2D materials. MOLs not only maintain the structural tunability and compositional diversity of MOFs but also have highly accessible active sites exposed on the external surface of 2D materials. MOLs offer the advantage of incorporating photosensitizers within their internal structure, enabling efficient light capture. Furthermore, by surface modification of MOLs with functional groups such as enzymes or metal nanoparticles, efficient catalytic activities can be achieved. MOLs exhibit notable advantages in the field of photocatalysis and have also shown promising performances in other fields. MOLs have exhibited significant potential in the field of separation processes [6]. The porous nature of MOFs enables selective adsorption and separation of molecules. By incorporating this characteristic into the two-dimensional structure of MOLs, it is possible to achieve highly optimized separation techniques, thereby enhancing the efficiency of processes such as gas separation and water treatment. Furthermore, MOLs have demonstrated promising applications in the domain of sensing [7,8]. The active sites present on the surface of MOLs can facilitate selective binding and detection of specific molecules or ions, rendering them attractive sensor materials for environmental monitoring, disease diagnosis, and other sensing applications. In the field of biomedicine, MOLs are being recognized for their potential as drug delivery systems. The tunable structure and high surface area of MOLs offer opportunities for efficient loading and controlled release of drugs [9–12]. Additionally, the functionalization of MOLs can enable the targeting of specific cells or tissues, thereby augmenting their potential as targeted drug delivery vehicles. Finally, MOLs have shown potential applications in catalysis [13–16]. The active sites on the surface of MOLs can act as catalytic centers for chemical reactions, and the organic ligand framework can be functionalized to introduce specific functional groups. By controlling the composition and structure of MOLs, researchers aim to develop catalysts with enhanced reactivity and selectivity for various industrial processes. In conclusion, MOLs show promising prospects in separation, sensing, biomedicine, and catalysis. Ongoing research endeavors aim to further comprehend and harness the potential applications of MOLs in these fields, utilizing their adjustable composition and structure.
In recent years, significant efforts have been focused on the design and synthesis of MOLs for applications in catalytic yields, particularly in the field of photocatalysis. Energy-converting systems for photoreactions generally contain two components: photosensitizers and catalysts. Photosensitizers can harvest the energy from the energy source (e.g., visible-light photons and X-ray photons) and transfer it to the catalysts [9,17–19]. Catalysts can convert and use this energy to generate high-energy molecules, such as reactive oxygen species (ROSs), fine chemicals, or solar fuels. The efficiency of energy and electron transfer between photosensitizers and catalysts is one of the most important factors for efficient photocatalysis [20].
Due to the aforementioned features of MOLs, the hierarchical integration of photosensitizers and catalysts separately into the ligand and surface of MOLs offers a promising approach for harnessing light energy and facilitating catalytic reactions. Within the integrated MOLs system, photosensitizers absorb photons, leading to the generation of excited states. These excited states can then undergo energy transfer to nearby catalytic sites present within the MOLs. The catalysts embedded within the MOLs can then utilize this harvested light energy to drive specific chemical reactions. As illustrated in Figure 1, the enhanced electron transfer from photoexcited ligands to catalysts in MOLs could be attributed to the proximity effect resulting from the co-localization of the light-sensitive ligands and photocatalysts within the MOLs structure. The integration of photosensitizers and catalysts in MOLs offers several advantages. First, compared with MOFs, MOLs can reduce the issues of light scattering due to their ultrathin morphology and dispersibility in solvents [21]. Meanwhile, the proximity of the photosensitizers and catalysts within the MOLs allows for efficient energy transfer and enhances the catalytic performance, which exhibits higher conversion yields and reaction rates under similar conditions when compared with homogeneous reaction systems [10]. Additionally, the tunable nature of MOLs enables the design and optimization of specific catalytic sites and light-harvesting capabilities, further enhancing the overall efficiency of the system.
MOLs also offer planar surfaces where substrates can directly interact with catalysts, and thus MOLs are not limited by the size or diffusion properties of substrates. This unrestricted access allows for the catalysis of substrates of various sizes and diffusion rates, enhancing the versatility and efficiency of the photocatalytic reactions. These advantages of MOLs provide promising prospects for the development of efficient and versatile photocatalytic systems. This review summarizes the progress of MOLs for photocatalysis, providing a deeper understanding of the structural design of MOLs-based catalysts with high performance.
## 2 Synthesis of photosensitizing MOLs
The synthesis of photosensitizing MOLs typically involves the controlled assembly of secondary building units (SBUs) and organic ligands under suitable reaction conditions. The choice of SBUs and ligand types, as well as their respective functional groups, can influence the photosensitivity of the resulting MOLs. Moreover, various synthetic strategies, such as solvothermal reactions, surfactant-assisted synthesis, and layer-by-layer exfoliation, have been explored to achieve MOLs with tailored structure and electronic properties [5]. Therefore, the rational design of the synthetic protocol and the molecular components are crucial for the successful preparation of photosensitizing MOLs with high efficiency and selectivity for target applications.
## 2.1 Structural design of Hf-MOLs
We take Hf-MOLs as a classic example to describe the topological structure of MOLs in this section. As shown in Figure 2, the ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{6}{\mathrm{-MOL}}$ is a 2D network formed by coordinating $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}$ secondary building units with triangular organic ligands, while the $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-MOL}}$ is constructed by coordinating $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ secondary building units with linear organic ligands.
In detail, for $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-MOL}}$ , ${\mathrm{Hf}}^{4+}$ ions and weakly coordinating trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) undergo hydrolysis to form $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters with TFA as modulators. Each $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ cluster exhibiting different lateral and vertical coordination abilities is formed by the coordination of 12 TFA groups laterally and 6 TFA groups axially. Linear organic ligands with high coordination affinity are chosen as the lateral bridging skeleton between $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters to promote the mechanical and chemical stability of materials. This double-layered connection, consisting of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters and linear organic ligands, facilitates the synergy of each layer of ligand. Moreover, with suitable ligands, lateral extension can be promoted, allowing for the formation of MOLs with various properties.
In ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{6}{\mathrm{-MOL}}$ , each $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}$ core is a hexagonal star-shaped structure formed by an organic ligand connecting three $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}$ clusters. The structure of $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}$ -MOL can be constructed into various stacking structures. However, the stacking of MOLs can be hindered by the presence of dangling-type ligands [22]. Nevertheless, by adjusting the reaction conditions and the choice of ligands, it is possible to exert control over the structure of ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{6}{\mathrm{-MOL}}$ and optimize its properties.
## 2.2 Synthetic strategy of $\mathbf{Hf}_{12}$ -MOLs
Strategies to synthesize MOLs can be categorized into two methods: top-down exfoliation and bottom-up assembly. MOLs prepared by different strategies usually exhibit different properties. Thus, target-oriented synthetic design is crucial for MOLs with high performance. Here, we offer $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -MOLs as an example to illustrate the synthetic strategy of MOLs (Figure 3). The solvothermal reaction is usually introduced into the Hf-MOLs synthesis system. In an optimized reaction system, the lateral-bound TFA groups on $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters are selectively replaced by the organic ligand to obtain an MOL structure that is laterally extended by the ligands and end-capped axially by weakly coordinating acids. The key to this method lies in exploiting the difference in the coordinative ability of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters in the lateral and axial directions, which is likely governed by the different charge distributions between the six interior and six axial Hf atoms of the $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters. This allows for the generation of
MOLs structures with well-defined morphologies and compositions.
## 2.3 Surface modification of $\mathbf{H}\mathbf{f}_{12}\mathbf{-M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{L}$
The surface modification of MOLs helps to achieve its multifunctionality. Through carboxylate exchange reactions, target molecules with carboxylic acid groups can replace the end-capping TFA and be modified on the surface of MOLs. Since TFA has weak coordination abilities with $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters, the modification process is relatively simple and can be achieved by stirring at room temperature overnight (Figure 4) [23].
## 3 Application of photosensitive MOLs
Based on the aforementioned surface modification, functional molecules can be installed onto the surface of the photosensitizing MOLs for various photoreactions, such as carbon dioxide reduction, $\mathsf{p H}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sensing, dehydrogenation reactions, cross-coupling reactions, Lewis acid reactions, and artificial photosynthesis [13,15,23–26]. Due to the efficient energy transfer between photosensitizers and catalysts in MOLs, these photochemical reactions catalyzed by MOLs are nearly one order of magnitude more active than their corresponding homogeneous reaction systems. In this review, we discussed three representative applications of MOLs to show the potential of MOLs in photoreactions.
## 3.1 MOLs for photocatalytic cross-coupling reactions
A multifunctional MOL, $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\mathrm{-Ir-Ni}$ , has been reported for highly efficient cross-coupling photocatalysis [15]. According to the previously mentioned synthesis strategy, the photosensitizers $(\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{DBB})[\mathrm{dF}(\mathrm{CF}_{3})\mathrm{ppy}]^{2+})$ are integrated into the MOL structure as ligands, and the catalysts $(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{MBA})\mathrm{Cl}_{2})$ are integrated at the vertical end of SBUs through carboxylate exchange.
The preparation of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-Ni MOL is described in detail as follows: Firstly, the secondary building units (SBUs) of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ clusters are vertically terminated with TFA capping agents possessing lower coordination ability, and are bridged by photosensitive DBB-Ir-F ligands, resulting in $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}(\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{O})_{8^{-}}$ $\mathrm{(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-OH)_{8}(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-OH)_{6}(D B B\mathrm{-}I r\mathrm{-}F)_{6}(T F A)_{6}}}}$ . Secondly, MBAs are introduced to the surface of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-F by replacing weakly coordinating TFA groups through carboxylate exchange to obtain $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-MBA. Finally, MBA capping agents on $\operatorname{Hf}_{12}.$ Ir-MBA are metalated to generate catalytically active sites (Figure 5). The proximity of photosensitizing ligands and catalysts leads to the reduction of the diffusion distance for electrons, which enhances energy transfer efficiency and catalytic performance. The diameter of the MOL capped with TFA is ${\sim}300~\mathrm{nm}$ , and its thickness is ${\sim}1.8\mathrm{nm}$ . After being capped with MBA, the thickness of the MOL increases to $2.8\mathrm{nm}$ by structure modeling (Figure 6).
Through atomic force microscopy (AFM) analysis, the experimental thickness of MOLs was consistent with the predicted value by modeling. The thickness of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-TFA was found to be $1.7\mathrm{nm}$ , while the thickness of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-MBANi was $2.9\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 7). This suggests a synthetic strategy for precise control of MOLs thickness, which involves the use of capping agents of different sizes. The thickness of the resulting MOL can be fine-tuned by selecting the appropriate capping agent.
The ultraviolet-visible (UV-Vis) and luminescence spectra showed that $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-TFA possesses absorption, excitation, and emission spectra similar to $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{DBB-Ir}$ , underscoring the photosensitizing capabilities of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-TFA. Furthermore, the absorption spectrum of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-MBA-Ni displayed characteristic peaks that correspond to both $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{DBB}$ -Ir-TFA and Me-MBA-Ni.
By incorporating photosensitizers within the MOLs, they can efficiently absorb and harness light energy, leading to enhanced photocatalytic performance. $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-MBA-Ni exhibited a significant enhancement in luminescence intensity quenching, with a 25-fold increase, and luminescence lifetime quenching, approximately an 8-fold increase, compared with the homogenous system (Figure 8). Simultaneously, a striking decrease in luminescence intensity was noted in $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-Ni as MBA-Ni loading increased. In contrast, the addition of Me-MBA-Ni to $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-F and $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{DBB-Ir-F}$ only resulted in moderate and minimal decreases in intensity, respectively. The luminescence intensity curves show strong alignment with theoretical forecasts, yielding Stern-Volmer constant $(K_{\mathrm{SV}})$ values of $3.01\pm0.15$ for $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-Ni, $0.63\pm0.06$ for $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ir-F, and $0.12\pm0.01$ for ${\bf M e}_{2}{\bf D B}{\bf B}$ -Ir-F.
The proximity $(\sim0.85\mathrm{nm}$ , based on the modeled structure) between photosensitizing bridging ligands and catalytic capping groups in ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-MOL}}$ suggests that electron transfer from the photoexcited DBB-Ir-F ligand to MBA- $\mathrm{.NiCl}_{2}$ would be highly efficient. The efficient electron transfer could significantly promote photocatalytic reactions, resulting in the improvement of catalytic performance. Moreover, due to the spatially isolated MBA-Ni sites on the MOL surface, the formation of Ni dimers, which have been shown to reduce photoredox reactivity, is prevented. It is beneficial to enhance the overall photocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -IrMBA-Ni. To evaluate its effectiveness, photocatalytic tests were conducted on three important single electron transfer (SET) reactions: C–S cross-coupling, C–O cross-coupling, and $\mathrm{C-C}$ cross-coupling (Figure 9). Throughout the study, $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-MOL}}$ with low levels of Ni loading exhibited remarkably efficient catalytic activity for all three specific cross-coupling reactions. The C–S cross-coupling reaction, for example, yielded turnover numbers (TONs) of $\mathord{\sim}4,500$ when utilizing an Ni loading concentration of just $0.02\mathrm{mol\%}$ . These high conversion rates represent a significant improvement compared with those achieved using homogenous conditions with the same molar ratio. In addition, the yield of the cross-coupling products increased by one to two orders of magnitude. Importantly, this typical $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}{\mathrm{-MOL}}$ demonstrated good stability and recyclability, which revealed great significance for sustainable development and production.
## 3.2 MOLs for ratiometric pH and oxygen sensing
With the continuous development of the biosensor field, optical probes have been increasingly utilized for sensing and imaging [27]. These biosensors play a vital role in early disease diagnosis by monitoring and imaging biological processes. By integrating optical probes into biosensor platforms, it is possible to achieve highly sensitive and selective detection of disease-related biomolecules. Therefore, this sort of sensor has great potential for advancing the field of biosensing and improving early disease diagnosis.
In the past decade, MOFs have been gradually introduced for biosensor applications [28]. However, as mentioned earlier, the intrinsic limitations of MOFs make it difficult to assemble complex molecular sensors and require certain sizes of analyte molecules, which hinders their development in the biosensor field. MOLs maintain the features of both MOFs and 2D materials. The integration of molecular sensors and probes into MOLs will undoubtedly broaden the development prospects of MOFs in the biosensor field and overcome their inherent deficiencies. In practice, the 2D structure of MOLs can increase the contact area with analytes, thereby enhancing the sensitivity and selectivity of sensors. Moreover, MOLs have good controllability to achieve precise design and construction of sensors by regulating their structure and composition. Thus, MOLs are a promising platform for further research and application in
the biosensor field.
Recently, ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-F/R}}$ MOL has been reported for monitoring physiological processes in mitochondria, particularly for ratiometric $\mathsf{p H}$ and oxygen sensing [24]. As shown in Figure 10, positively charged $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru MOL is capable of specifically targeting mitochondria and enabling ratiometric sensing of intra-mitochondrial $\mathsf{p H}$ and oxygen. During this process, the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -sensitive DBB-Ru was used as organic ligands and incorporated into the MOLs for directed molecular recognition. Moreover, the pH-sensitive fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) and pH-independent rhodamine-B isothiocyanate (RITC) were assembled onto $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ SBUs through carboxylate exchange reactions to synthesize ratiometric sensor Hf12-Ru-F/R for monitoring $\mathsf{p H}$ and oxygen, respectively.
In the experiment, the loading concentrations of FITC and RITC in the $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru MOL were optimized to ensure comparable luminescence intensities for FITC, RITC, and DBBRu. At the specified loading concentrations, luminescence intensities for DBB-Ru were measured using an excitation wavelength of $430\mathrm{nm}$ and an emission wavelength of $620\mathrm{nm}$ . FITC, on the other hand, was measured at excitation/emission wavelengths of $465/500\mathrm{nm}$ , while RITC was measured at $535/600\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 11a). Fluorimetry revealed that only FITC fluorescence intensities exhibited significant changes under normoxia and $\mathrm{pH}$ value of $4.0\substack{-8.0}$ conditions (Figure 11b). The response of Hf-MOL was similar to that of free FITC and well-fitted with the theoretical formula. In addition, as shown in Figure 11c, under varied $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ partial pressures, only DBB-Ru demonstrated noteworthy luminescence intensity variations while the other two dyes remained unchanged. Subsequently, fluorescence signals of FITC, RITC, and DBB-Ru in MOLs were separately measured by an IVIS imaging system. The results demonstrated that FITC only responded to pH, DBB-Ru only responded to oxygen, and RITC showed no response to either $\mathsf{p H}$ or oxygen.
This study further assessed the applicability of ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-MOL}}$ as a sensor in living cells by confocal scanning laser microscopy (CLSM). It was found that, under anoxic conditions, the observed signals indicated a simultaneous decrease in oxygen pressure and $\mathfrak{p H}$ , while cells displayed a bright magenta signal of DBB-Ru and a dim green signal of FITC in high-res CLSM images. In contrast, under normal oxygen conditions, both oxygen pressure and $\mathsf{p H}$ values were relatively increased. The analysis of over 300 regions of interests (ROIs) found a positive correlation between $\mathsf{p H}$ and local oxygen concentration inside mitochondria, suggesting that changes in $\mathfrak{p H}$ are closely related to changes in oxygen concentration (Figure 12). This quantitative investigation provides novel insights into the relationship between $\mathsf{p H}$ and oxygen concentration in mitochondria, which may have important implications for understanding the cellular processes that rely on these two critical factors.
In summary, ${\mathrm{Hf}}_{12}{\mathrm{-Ru-F/R}}$ formed by integrating FITC and RITC onto the surface of $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}$ -Ru MOL, as a ratiometric sensor, is used for high-resolution confocal microscope imaging to quantify the relationship between $\mathrm{\pH}$ and local $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration in mitochondria firstly. The integration exhibits potential for biomedical applications, particularly in the context of monitoring mitochondrial function during metabolic diseases and cellular metabolism. It helps to improve targeting efficiency for mitochondrial measurements, enabling more accurate and sensitive monitoring of physiological processes within this organelle.
Therefore, this technology represents a novel and potentially effective approach for the investigation of complex biological processes and the development of more efficient treatments in the future.
## 3.3 MOLs for artificial photosynthesis
Metalloenzymes are effective and multifunctional biocatalysts with high reactivity and selectivity due to their complex active sites, which are composed of a metal ion coordinated to amino acid (AA) side chains or a prosthetic group, secondary coordination to nearby AA side chains, and other associated cofactors. The metal ion located within the center of the active site is primarily responsible for catalytic activity and its interaction with specifically tailored ligands and environmental factors around it. By studying the intricate mechanisms underlying metalloenzymes, we can gain a deeper understanding of how to design synthetic catalysts that effectively mimic their unique reactivity and selectivity [29]. Additionally, metalloenzymes play critical roles in various biological functions, such as DNA repair and oxygen transport, and understanding their functions is crucial in developing treatments for enzyme-related diseases [30]. Many materials, such as metal clusters [31–33], MOFs [34,35], and metal and metal oxide nanoparticles [36], have been designed and investigated for the construction of artificial systems. However, these artificial enzymes mainly mimic the active metal centers of enzymes and lack system adjustability, which may limit the expansion of catalytic systems.
Based on the carboxylate exchange surface modification of MOLs, not only can we alter their intrinsic properties such as morphology, porosity, and functionality, but we can also fine-tune their surface properties, such as hydrophobicity, electrostatic charge, and chemical reactivity. This surface modification process enhances MOL interactions with diverse environments, facilitates targeted chemical reactions, and improves their versatility. Recently, a new class of artificial enzymes based on MOLs, referred to MOZs, have been reported by the precise arrangement of metal centers, AAs, and pigments to generate complex active sites (Figure 13a) [23]. Metal catalysts and amino acid residues were anchored onto the surface of Hf clusters to mimic the metal center and the secondary coordination environment of enzyme active centers, respectively. After that, as shown in Figure 13b, the optimization of MOZs proceeds through three steps: first, construction of diverse MOZs template by the integration of AAs; second, selection of MOZs for high performance; third, optimization activity and exploration of the mechanism of high activity for the design of artificial ligand.
Since the surface modification of MOLs is straightforward, high-throughput screening of MOZs can be easily achieved. We take the design of an MOZ library for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ as an example to illustrate in detail (Figure 14).
Hf-Ir MOL was synthesized through a solvothermal action first. Then, the design of MOZ-1 was achieved by the integration of the ferric protoporphyrin hemin with an active Fe-based center onto Hf-If MOL through carboxylate exchange. A series of AAs was utilized for the construction of library MOZs with functional diversity. MOZ-2 (with Glu modification) and MOZ-3 (with Asn modification) were next selected for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ from the library of MOZs. Finally, MOZ-4 with the highest activity was obtained by cooperating Ur-ligand inspired by hydrogenbonding interaction in MOZ-3 onto MOZ-1.
The activity evaluation of MOZs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ by quantifying the generation of CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ revealed that MOZs modified by AAs can enhance $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ activity, which is attributed to two pathways: proton-coupled electron transfer (PCET) and hydrogen-bond (H-bond) stabilization (Figure 14a). Furthermore, MOZ-4 with the highest activity was recycled at least five cycles with no obvious loss of activity (Figure 15b). Importantly, compared with previously reported work, MOZs in this work exhibited higher selectivity.
Except for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ , an MOZ library for WOR was constructed and optimized by the same strategy. The library of MOZs was also diversified by modifying AA onto the primary MOZs and selected for photocatalytic WOR (Figure 16a). As a result, MOZ-7 significantly enhanced the activity of WOR with a $72\mathrm{{h}}$ TON for $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ generation of $8,679\pm446$ (Figure 16b). By combing the MOZ-4 and MOZ7 together, photocatalytic total $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ was realized through a Z-scheme with the turnover frequency (TOF) optimized to $98.7\pm3.7\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , which is the highest TOF number reported.
In summary, a variety of MOZs based on MOLs is designed through the integration active centers, AAs, and other cofactors onto MOLs and shows high performance for artificial photocatalysis. Thus, MOLs with high densities of open sites to allow the cooperation of complex molecules exhibit enormous potential for the system construction of artificial enzyme applications in photocatalysis.
## 4 Conclusions and outlooks
In conclusion, MOLs have emerged as an exciting class of materials with immense potential in various fields. This review has provided an overview of the structural design and synthesis strategies of MOLs, focusing on their application in photocatalysis, particularly in artificial photocatalysis. By integrating targeted molecules into MOLs, higher reaction rates and conversion efficiencies can be achieved compared with conventional homogeneous systems.
However, it is important to note that MOLs are still in the early stages of development, and several challenges need to be addressed to fully harness their characterics. There is a need for the development of more standardized and universal synthesis methods. As a relatively new field of research, understanding the precise mechanisms behind MOL growth is still a subject of ongoing investigation. Several factors contribute to the complexity of studying MOLs growth when employing the bottom-up approach. Firstly, the dynamic nature of MOLs formation makes it difficult to capture the intermediates and pathways involved in the growth process. MOLs can form through various synthetic approaches, such as solvothermal or hydrothermal methods, and the conditions during synthesis can influence the resulting structures. The precise interactions between metal ions and organic ligands, as well as their assembly into organized structures, are still not fully understood. Secondly, characterizing the growth of MOLs at the molecular level presents challenges. Techniques such as X-ray crystallography and electron microscopy provide valuable information about the final structures of MOLs. However, understanding the step-by-step growth process requires techniques with a higher spatial and temporal resolution to capture intermediate stages.
Future research on MOLs should focus on expanding their application scope beyond photocatalysis. In chemistry, MOLs can serve as versatile platforms for catalysis, enabling the development of efficient and selective catalysts for chemical reactions. MOLs can also find applications in drug delivery and imaging in biology. In medicine, MOLs hold promise in areas such as tissue engineering and regenerative medicine. They can be used as scaffolds to support cell growth and tissue formation. By functionalizing MOLs with bioactive molecules and controlling their surface properties, researchers can create platforms that promote cell adhesion, proliferation, and differentiation, ultimately aiding in tissue repair and regeneration. In summary, MOLs offer unique opportunities for the development of functional materials with tailored properties. Furthermore, MOLs can be easily separated from the reaction mixture or solution. This feature enables efficient purification processes, reduces waste generation, and aligns with environmentally friendly and sustainable material development.
